00:01:24.776 --> 00:01:27.480
- [Voiceover] Iwona asks,
"How many punches in the face

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:29.873
and failures can an ordinary person handle

00:01:29.873 --> 00:01:32.050
before achieving success?"

00:01:32.479 --> 00:01:36.224
- Iwona, the right word in that
question is ordinary person.

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:37.855
What was it, an ordinary person?

00:01:37.855 --> 00:01:38.819
Yeah, and I think that's

00:01:38.819 --> 00:01:40.140
the interesting part of your question.

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:41.941
Which is this whole
notion that pisses me off

00:01:41.941 --> 00:01:44.100
that I don't believe that the far majority

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:45.633
of people right now who claim that

00:01:45.633 --> 00:01:47.733
they're entrepreneurs are entrepreneurs.

00:01:47.733 --> 00:01:49.522
You know, I don't get to claim that I'm

00:01:49.522 --> 00:01:52.157
an NFL quarterback,
and then I just am one,

00:01:52.157 --> 00:01:53.736
and that's what's happening, right?

00:01:53.736 --> 00:01:55.315
A lot of people that
don't have the skills,

00:01:55.315 --> 00:01:58.878
you put that word ordinary
in for a very specific reason

00:01:58.878 --> 00:02:00.921
and it's the reason I want to rant on this

00:02:00.921 --> 00:02:03.835
which is the ordinary person a.k.a.

00:02:03.835 --> 00:02:06.331
the person that's not
meant to run a business

00:02:06.331 --> 00:02:09.210
can probably only handle one punch, right?

00:02:09.210 --> 00:02:11.230
I mean like, that's just
what it comes down to.

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:12.913
You know what my answer is, unlimited.

00:02:12.913 --> 00:02:15.118
You could punch me in the face 8000 times.

00:02:15.118 --> 00:02:17.152
I'm here to get punched, right?

00:02:17.152 --> 00:02:18.474
Like, you know I really do think

00:02:18.474 --> 00:02:19.832
of it like a UFC or a boxer.

00:02:19.832 --> 00:02:22.397
Have you ever watched
a UFC or boxing match,

00:02:22.397 --> 00:02:24.592
and literally watched
and thought to yourself

00:02:24.592 --> 00:02:26.936
holy crap, if I took one of those punches

00:02:26.936 --> 00:02:28.899
I'd be in a coma for the rest of my life.

00:02:28.899 --> 00:02:30.734
They're meant to be in the octagon,

00:02:30.734 --> 00:02:32.741
I am not.

00:02:32.741 --> 00:02:35.121
On the flip side, you show me a world

00:02:35.121 --> 00:02:37.698
where all 420 of these wonderful and

00:02:37.698 --> 00:02:40.020
amazing people quit VaynerMedia,

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:41.982
and I know exactly what
to do the next day.

00:02:41.982 --> 00:02:43.306
That's how I roll.

00:02:43.306 --> 00:02:44.977
Those are the punches I can handle.

00:02:44.977 --> 00:02:46.382
Top 10 clients quit, cool.

00:02:46.382 --> 00:02:49.123
Can't ship to a state at
Wine Library anymore, cool.

00:02:49.123 --> 00:02:51.746
I can handle unlimited punches because

00:02:51.746 --> 00:02:54.636
I'm pure-bred 100% entrepreneur.

00:02:54.636 --> 00:02:58.956
And so for me, to a person
that is a wannabe-preneur,

00:02:58.956 --> 00:03:02.240
who first punch in's like eh,
I'm going to go get a job.

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:06.524
From there, everybody fits
somewhere in between that.

00:03:06.524 --> 00:03:08.433
And that's your answer.

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:12.323
- [Voiceover] N asks, "Any
tips on how to get a mentor?"

00:03:13.838 --> 00:03:16.160
- This is an interesting question for me

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:19.654
because I've never, like
even when I had a mentor

00:03:19.654 --> 00:03:23.033
in my amazing dad, I like
pushed against it because...

00:03:23.033 --> 00:03:25.238
My mom and dad like, made jokes, like,

00:03:25.238 --> 00:03:28.094
that I want to be claimed and like I'm a

00:03:28.094 --> 00:03:30.033
Cabbage Patch kid and I came from nowhere.

00:03:30.033 --> 00:03:32.121
My dad always like to rouse
me when he thinks he deserves

00:03:32.121 --> 00:03:34.419
more credit because I
never wanted to have that.

00:03:34.419 --> 00:03:37.714
It's actually probably one
of my biggest weaknesses

00:03:37.714 --> 00:03:41.394
is my lack of mentorship
or learning from others,

00:03:41.394 --> 00:03:43.542
like I don't know, I like
being self contained.

00:03:43.542 --> 00:03:45.318
So I'm not the best person
to ask this question.

00:03:45.318 --> 00:03:48.035
As a matter of fact, I'm pretty
sucky to ask this question

00:03:48.035 --> 00:03:50.577
but I have answered it in the past,

00:03:50.577 --> 00:03:53.583
and I think we've touched on
this theme on the show before

00:03:53.583 --> 00:03:55.754
which is I think you just
got to go and get it, right?

00:03:55.754 --> 00:03:58.053
Like, and there's some people
doing it right now like

00:03:58.053 --> 00:03:59.771
that one dude that wants
to get you fired, Steve.

00:03:59.771 --> 00:04:01.385
I like that dude's bravado,

00:04:01.385 --> 00:04:02.709
he's like how do I get Steve's job?

00:04:02.709 --> 00:04:03.985
And like he e-mailed me, you see what

00:04:03.985 --> 00:04:05.146
I'm talking about these
last couple of days?

00:04:05.146 --> 00:04:06.329
- [Steve] Oh yeah.
- Yeah, what's his name?

00:04:06.329 --> 00:04:08.001
- [Steve] Did you see my Tweet storm?

00:04:08.001 --> 00:04:10.847
- Yeah, and so like, you
know, that's interesting to me

00:04:10.847 --> 00:04:12.250
because it keeps pounding
me and eventually

00:04:12.250 --> 00:04:14.086
I'm going to know his name because

00:04:14.086 --> 00:04:15.571
he's like I want to be mentored.

00:04:15.571 --> 00:04:17.440
Now the truth is I'm not
looking for that right now.

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:18.809
I don't feel like I can deliver.

00:04:18.809 --> 00:04:21.466
I can only deliver a mentorship
through osmosis, right?

00:04:21.466 --> 00:04:23.407
We as a collective, had
a meeting earlier today

00:04:23.407 --> 00:04:26.181
and I think the youngsters all
picked up a little something

00:04:26.181 --> 00:04:28.027
that they'll use and they'll
like learn of like how to scale

00:04:28.027 --> 00:04:31.869
but I don't want like a
hey, come here Johnny,

00:04:31.869 --> 00:04:33.740
I going to... I don't want that.

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:35.725
There's people that do,
and I think the best way

00:04:35.725 --> 00:04:38.604
to get one is just to keep
asking the 5 to 7 people

00:04:38.604 --> 00:04:40.646
that you think can
deliver on that for you.

00:04:40.646 --> 00:04:43.979
Basically get to the point
of a restraining order

00:04:43.979 --> 00:04:45.707
without going over that line.

00:04:45.707 --> 00:04:48.459
Sorry Steve, but like
I really believe that.

00:04:48.459 --> 00:04:49.968
Steve just did this.

00:04:49.968 --> 00:04:51.860
I really, really believe that.

00:04:51.860 --> 00:04:53.602
You know, if you're
listening on the podcast,

00:04:53.602 --> 00:04:55.343
like you know, Steve
just put palm to head.

00:04:55.343 --> 00:04:57.921
But I believe that, I think
you have to go and get yours

00:04:57.921 --> 00:04:59.859
but freak a girl out, you know.

00:04:59.859 --> 00:05:01.136
You can't be, like, a stalker.

00:05:01.136 --> 00:05:04.143
You got to go and ask that
person and you ask them,

00:05:04.143 --> 00:05:06.082
and the other way to do it is to

00:05:06.082 --> 00:05:08.102
provide that person value first.

00:05:08.102 --> 00:05:10.226
The amount of people that
hit me up for mentorship

00:05:10.226 --> 00:05:13.824
where they hit me up, DRock!

00:05:13.824 --> 00:05:16.286
Right behind the camera right now.

00:05:16.286 --> 00:05:20.547
Hey, I want to make a
long form piece of content

00:05:20.547 --> 00:05:25.547
for you, on me, you know,
Cloud and Dirt, link it up.

00:05:25.770 --> 00:05:28.440
You know, what that led
to is what we have now.

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:32.371
I mean, I'm baffled by
people's lack of pains.

00:05:32.371 --> 00:05:34.891
You want something so
amazing from somebody

00:05:34.891 --> 00:05:37.189
which is their time and their energy

00:05:37.189 --> 00:05:39.233
and your opening question
to that person is

00:05:39.233 --> 00:05:41.253
hey, can you give it to me?

00:05:41.253 --> 00:05:44.189
That is insanity, how do
you provide value, Alex.

00:05:44.189 --> 00:05:45.849
I mean this whole room is full of...

00:05:45.849 --> 00:05:48.473
Alex takes a step in a different direction

00:05:48.473 --> 00:05:50.272
in his entrepreneurial
career, willing to come in

00:05:50.272 --> 00:05:52.594
as a community manager, show his face!

00:05:52.594 --> 00:05:55.090
Show this man's face when this happens.

00:05:55.090 --> 00:05:57.692
Willing to come in at
entry-level job with the hope

00:05:57.692 --> 00:06:01.046
that hey, I hope that
I get noticed and then

00:06:01.046 --> 00:06:03.316
I can get into that inner
circle that Gary has.

00:06:03.316 --> 00:06:06.073
And when he was noticed and brought in,

00:06:06.073 --> 00:06:09.173
he said, you know I don't
want to put you out here,

00:06:09.173 --> 00:06:11.495
but like, man I can't
believe this happened so much

00:06:11.495 --> 00:06:14.570
sooner which, it happened
what, 4 or 5 months in?

00:06:14.570 --> 00:06:17.576
3 months in, when he says so much sooner,

00:06:17.576 --> 00:06:19.435
he was in the, I don't
want to speak for you,

00:06:19.435 --> 00:06:21.373
18 months, 25, when were you going to

00:06:21.373 --> 00:06:23.276
give up at not getting noticed?

00:06:23.276 --> 00:06:26.049
- I was never giving up but
a year's time was my focus.

00:06:26.049 --> 00:06:27.912
- So he would have gave up after a year.

00:06:27.912 --> 00:06:29.176
(laughter)

00:06:29.176 --> 00:06:30.650
But that's the key, right?

00:06:30.650 --> 00:06:32.172
Like, he came in and he
wanted to pay it forward

00:06:32.172 --> 00:06:33.774
and his version of paying it forward

00:06:33.774 --> 00:06:35.537
and being in my ecosystem
was coming to Vayner,

00:06:35.537 --> 00:06:38.591
start at the bottom, work
it to the top, you know.

00:06:38.591 --> 00:06:41.215
And so, you know, you want a mentor?

00:06:41.215 --> 00:06:43.072
Why don't you provide
that mentor with so much

00:06:43.072 --> 00:06:45.416
upfront value that you guilt her or him

00:06:45.416 --> 00:06:47.838
into mentoring you.

00:06:47.855 --> 00:06:49.493
- Hey Gary, Sean Bruce here.
- My boy!

00:06:49.493 --> 00:06:51.337
- We've had a couple
opportunities to talk before.

00:06:51.337 --> 00:06:52.695
I do the sales and marketing for

00:06:52.695 --> 00:06:53.995
the Lancaster Hummus Company.

00:06:53.995 --> 00:06:56.155
My question today is what
was the biggest decision

00:06:56.155 --> 00:06:57.676
you made in your life that made

00:06:57.676 --> 00:06:59.313
you as successful as you are today?

00:06:59.313 --> 00:07:01.391
I've watched a lot of your
keynotes. I've watched a lot

00:07:01.391 --> 00:07:03.573
of your different rants
and ways and everything

00:07:03.573 --> 00:07:05.512
and interviews, but I
want to know what is your

00:07:05.512 --> 00:07:07.509
honest opinion on the biggest thing

00:07:07.509 --> 00:07:10.016
that you did that made you as
successful as you are today.

00:07:10.016 --> 00:07:11.874
Thanks Gary, and I look
forward to your answer.

00:07:11.874 --> 00:07:13.762
- Thanks brother.

00:07:14.394 --> 00:07:17.169
Man, I'm so pissed that
technology wasn't around.

00:07:17.169 --> 00:07:19.351
I would have been able to...

00:07:19.351 --> 00:07:21.451
Is this was around when I
was around, we would have

00:07:21.451 --> 00:07:23.645
been able to play me
as a 14 year old asking

00:07:23.645 --> 00:07:26.711
some entrepreneur that
question on YouTube.

00:07:26.711 --> 00:07:28.898
So pissed, anyway!

00:07:30.594 --> 00:07:32.822
You know, I'm glad you
asked this question because

00:07:32.822 --> 00:07:36.003
I now can really, like, I
was going there in my mind.

00:07:36.003 --> 00:07:38.419
I know what the answer is,
it's a weird answer actually.

00:07:38.419 --> 00:07:40.578
I think the biggest
decision I ever made was

00:07:40.578 --> 00:07:42.783
in fourth grade when I
got an F on a science

00:07:42.783 --> 00:07:47.329
test in Mr. Mulnar's class,

00:07:47.858 --> 00:07:50.980
and I decided literally after, you know,

00:07:50.980 --> 00:07:53.812
first I hid the, I had to
get it signed by my mom.

00:07:53.812 --> 00:07:56.354
I don't know if they still do
that when you get bad grades,

00:07:56.354 --> 00:07:57.958
like this was some 80's stuff.

00:07:57.958 --> 00:07:59.954
But, yeah they're doing it?

00:07:59.954 --> 00:08:01.966
I had to get it signed
and I was not interested

00:08:01.966 --> 00:08:03.905
in being punished, so I
didn't bring it to her.

00:08:03.905 --> 00:08:06.889
Then I put it under my bed and
then it sat there for 2 days

00:08:06.889 --> 00:08:08.803
but then my, I was still
young, my conscience

00:08:08.803 --> 00:08:10.626
still had too much power
and like, I got scared

00:08:10.626 --> 00:08:12.368
and told my mom about it.

00:08:12.368 --> 00:08:14.306
By the way, three years
later I was flushing every

00:08:14.306 --> 00:08:16.268
report card directly down the toilet.

00:08:16.268 --> 00:08:18.102
You can evolve quickly.

00:08:18.102 --> 00:08:20.309
When you make the mental
decision that I made,

00:08:20.309 --> 00:08:22.583
no joke I literally
remember sitting in my room

00:08:22.583 --> 00:08:25.486
and having a weird, weird
kind of like crying,

00:08:25.486 --> 00:08:27.505
debating, like moment in fourth grade

00:08:27.505 --> 00:08:29.781
in my small bedroom deciding

00:08:29.781 --> 00:08:34.110
screw school, I'm a business
man, and I'm going to eat

00:08:34.110 --> 00:08:36.908
the pain of being punished
every four times a year

00:08:36.908 --> 00:08:39.811
during the school year,
being viewed upon as a loser

00:08:39.811 --> 00:08:43.246
or a kid that doesn't have
a shot by all of society

00:08:43.246 --> 00:08:45.581
because I see something different.

00:08:45.581 --> 00:08:47.787
I have enough self awareness of who I am,

00:08:47.787 --> 00:08:50.840
I'm going to win and
literally, and this is weird

00:08:50.840 --> 00:08:52.627
as I'm a fourth grader and
you know you're young...

00:08:52.627 --> 00:08:54.404
how old are you in the fourth grade?

00:08:54.404 --> 00:08:59.404
9, 10, you know, and I'm literally
deciding that I'm willing

00:08:59.406 --> 00:09:02.739
to eat it for the next 8
years maybe even 12 years of

00:09:02.739 --> 00:09:06.060
my life where, that was
just a hardcore decision.

00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:08.660
And it's not that I didn't care,
like I went to every class.

00:09:08.660 --> 00:09:11.446
I just decided to hone in on my skills.

00:09:11.446 --> 00:09:13.628
That I would learn more
about selling baseball cards

00:09:13.628 --> 00:09:15.266
that later, at 14, became I would

00:09:15.266 --> 00:09:17.843
learn more about selling wine, I honed in.

00:09:17.843 --> 00:09:21.081
And so, it was the first
time, my man, that I made

00:09:21.081 --> 00:09:24.726
a decision that I was going
to fight society's optics

00:09:24.726 --> 00:09:27.756
and deliver on what I thought
I was, and that's what I did.

